meta {
  name: Introspect Token
  type: http
  seq: 9
}

post {
  url: {{auth_url}}/connect/introspection
  body: formUrlEncoded
  auth: none
}

body:form-urlencoded {
  token: {{machine_access_token}}
  client_id: {{machine_client_id}}
  client_secret: {{machine_client_secret}}
}

docs {
  RFC 7662, for callers that can't validate JWTs locally: is this token live, and whose is it —
  from the server's mouth. Run "Client Credentials (Machine)" first to stash
  {{machine_access_token}}.

  The posture, all of it provable from here:
  - Confidential clients only — swap client_id to `bruno` (no secret) and the endpoint refuses
    with unauthorized_client before looking at the token.
  - A caller sees the real answer only for a token it presented or is an audience of; any other
    token — try pasting {{access_token}} in — answers `active: false`, so a stolen token can't
    be probed here.
  - Claim details like scope go to a token's audiences only; a mere presenter gets liveness and
    metadata.
}
